[32m2026-11-21 09:44:54[0m | [34m[1mDEBUG[0m | "./app/services/task.py:376":[34m generate_script[0m - [34m[1mvideo script: 
It starts in the morning. You're in the bedroom, and you think: I need my keys. You walk into the hallway, then the kitchen. And the thought is gone. You've done this. Almost everyone has. And for years it looked like simple absent-mindedness. Then a team of memory researchers decided to test it. They set up rooms in a lab. People picked up an object at one table, carried it, and put it down at another table. Sometimes the second table was in the same big room. Sometimes it was through a door, in a new room. The walking distance was the same. Then, right after they put it down, they were asked a simple question: what did you just put down? The people who had walked through a doorway forgot more. Same time. Same distance. The only difference was the door. The researchers then tried something else. If the memory was left behind in the first room, maybe going back would bring it back. That's what we all do, right? Walk back to the bedroom and suddenly remember. In a virtual version of the test, people who walked back into the first room did not do any better. What mattered was how many new rooms they had entered. Each new room made the forgetting a little worse. So what's going on? The best explanation is that your brain doesn't record your day as one long video. It cuts it into scenes, a bit like a film editor. A doorway is a strong signal that a scene has ended. When the scene ends, your brain files the old scene away and gets ready for the new one. The plan you made in the bedroom gets filed with the bedroom. That's not a bug. Think about how much you do in a day. If every plan from every room stayed active, your head would be full of old, finished business. Closing the scene keeps your attention on where you are now. The cost is small: sometimes you lose a plan you still needed. And this is the first clue to our question. Your brain isn't trying to keep everything. It's trying to keep what fits the moment you're in. But the next part of your day is harder on memory, because now there are other people in the room.[0m
